IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `fraud-nouns`

-- Fraud Act 2006 (2006 c. 35), SCHEDULE 2: TRANSITIONAL PROVISIONS AND SAVINGS.
--
-- Paragraph 1 (the penalty term for offences before 2 May 2022) is stated once, with the penalty
-- subsections it modifies, in fraud-penalty.l4, and the date it fixes is in fraud-dates.l4.
-- Paragraph 2 is inert (see the foot of this file).  Paragraphs 3 to 11 are here.
--
-- Schedule 1 paragraph 1 abolished the old deception offences (Theft Acts 1968 and 1978 and the
-- Northern Ireland equivalents).  Paragraph 3 saves liability for such an offence that was PARTLY
-- committed before the abolition took effect.  The text says nothing about an offence committed
-- WHOLLY before the abolition: that is left to other law (the Act does not say), and the rule
-- below returns that answer as a named value and does not guess.
--
-- Paragraphs 4 to 10 each say that one amendment made by Schedule 1 does not affect the operation
-- of a provision of another Act where the goods, credit or chattels were obtained in the
-- circumstances the old section described and the deception (or credit) came BEFORE the
-- commencement of that paragraph of Schedule 1.  The other Acts are not among the sources, so
-- "in the circumstances described" is a fact the person states, and the day each paragraph of
-- Schedule 1 came into force is an input (section 15(1): by order, different days for
-- different purposes).

§ `Schedule 2: transitional provisions and savings`

§§ `Paragraph 3: abolition of deception offences`

-- src:347 | Abolition of deception offences
-- src:348 | 3 (1) Paragraph 1 of Schedule 1 does not affect any liability, investigation, legal proceeding or penalty for or in respect of any offence partly committed before the commencement of that paragraph.
-- src:349 | (2) An offence is partly committed before the commencement of paragraph 1 of Schedule 1 if—
-- src:350 | (a) a relevant event occurs before its commencement, and
-- src:351 | (b) another relevant event occurs on or after its commencement.
-- src:352 | (3) “Relevant event”, in relation to an offence, means any act, omission or other event (including any result of one or more acts or omissions) proof of which is required for conviction of the offence.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 Schedule 2 paragraph 3(2) and paragraph 11(2) (src:349-351)
GIVEN events IS A LIST OF DATE
      commencement IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Schedule 2 paragraphs 3(2) and 11(2): the offence was partly committed before the commencement` events commencement MEANS
    -- Paragraphs 3(3) and 11(3) define a "relevant event" in the same words: any act, omission or
    -- other event (including any result of one or more acts or omissions) proof of which is
    -- required for conviction.  `events` holds the day each relevant event occurred.  Partly
    -- committed: (a) one relevant event before the commencement, and (b) another on or after it.
        any (GIVEN day YIELD day LESS THAN commencement) events
    AND any (GIVEN day YIELD day AT LEAST commencement) events

-- src:148 | Section 14(1)
-- src:149 | Abolition of various deception offences
-- src:150 | 1 Omit the following provisions—
@ref Fraud Act 2006 Schedule 1 paragraph 1 and Schedule 2 paragraph 3(1) (src:150) (src:348)
GIVEN events IS A LIST OF DATE
      commencement IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Outcome for an old deception offence`
`Schedule 2 paragraph 3(1): what becomes of liability for an old deception offence` events commencement MEANS
    -- `commencement` is the day paragraph 1 of Schedule 1 came into force.
    BRANCH
        IF `Schedule 2 paragraphs 3(2) and 11(2): the offence was partly committed before the commencement` events commencement
        THEN `liability is saved by paragraph 3 of Schedule 2`
        IF (NOT (events EQUALS EMPTY)) AND all (GIVEN day YIELD day AT LEAST commencement) events
        THEN `the offence was abolished before any of its relevant events: no liability under it`
        OTHERWISE `the Act does not say (every relevant event was before commencement, or none is given)`

§§ `Paragraphs 4 to 10: savings for provisions of other Acts`

-- src:353 | Scope of offences relating to stolen goods under the Theft Act 1968 (c. 60)
-- src:354 | 4 Nothing in paragraph 6 of Schedule 1 affects the operation of section 24 of the Theft Act 1968 in relation to goods obtained in the circumstances described in section 15(1) of that Act where the obtaining is the result of a deception made before the commencement of that paragraph.
-- src:355 | Dishonestly retaining a wrongful credit under the Theft Act 1968
-- src:356 | 5 Nothing in paragraph 7 of Schedule 1 affects the operation of section 24A(7) and (8) of the Theft Act 1968 in relation to credits falling within section 24A(3) or (4) of that Act and made before the commencement of that paragraph.
-- src:357 | Scope of offences relating to stolen goods under the Theft Act (Northern Ireland) 1969 (c. 16 (N.I.))
-- src:358 | 6 Nothing in paragraph 11 of Schedule 1 affects the operation of section 23 of the Theft Act (Northern Ireland) 1969 in relation to goods obtained in the circumstances described in section 15(1) of that Act where the obtaining is the result of a deception made before the commencement of that paragraph.
-- src:359 | Dishonestly retaining a wrongful credit under the Theft Act (Northern Ireland) 1969
-- src:360 | 7 Nothing in paragraph 12 of Schedule 1 affects the operation of section 23A(7) and (8) of the Theft Act (Northern Ireland) 1969 in relation to credits falling within section 23A(3) or (4) of that Act and made before the commencement of that paragraph.
-- src:361 | Limitation periods under the Limitation Act 1980 (c. 58)
-- src:362 | 8 Nothing in paragraph 18 of Schedule 1 affects the operation of section 4 of the Limitation Act 1980 in relation to chattels obtained in the circumstances described in section 15(1) of the Theft Act 1968 where the obtaining is a result of a deception made before the commencement of that paragraph.
-- src:363 | Limitation periods under the Limitation (Northern Ireland) Order 1989 (S.I. 1989/1339 (N.I. 11))
-- src:364 | 9 Nothing in paragraph 22 of Schedule 1 affects the operation of Article 18 of the Limitation (Northern Ireland) Order 1989 in relation to chattels obtained in the circumstances described in section 15(1) of the Theft Act (Northern Ireland) 1969 where the obtaining is a result of a deception made before the commencement of that paragraph.
-- src:365 | Scheduled offences under the Terrorism Act 2000 (c. 11)
-- src:366 | 10 Nothing in paragraph 30 of Schedule 1 affects the operation of Part 7 of the Terrorism Act 2000 in relation to an offence under section 15(1) of the Theft Act (Northern Ireland) 1969 where the obtaining is a result of a deception made before the commencement of that paragraph.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 Schedule 2 paragraphs 4 to 10 (src:354) (src:356) (src:358) (src:360) (src:362) (src:364) (src:366)
GIVEN `the circumstances described in the old provision apply` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the deception, or the credit, was made` IS A DATE
      `the day the paragraph of Schedule 1 came into force` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Schedule 2 paragraphs 4 to 10: the saving applies` `the circumstances described in the old provision apply` `the day the deception, or the credit, was made` `the day the paragraph of Schedule 1 came into force` MEANS
    -- The shape common to the seven paragraphs: the old circumstances apply AND the deception (or
    -- credit) was made before the commencement of that paragraph of Schedule 1.  "Before" is strict.
        `the circumstances described in the old provision apply`
    AND `the day the deception, or the credit, was made` LESS THAN `the day the paragraph of Schedule 1 came into force`

